## Earlier request: correct single-scroll double stepping and first-move pacing

On 2026-10-24 the user reported that one scroll was moving two sections and
making the first transition too fast. The earlier 50ms classifier was wrong for
gapped momentum: one decaying stream `[180,120,80,45,24,12,5]` at 80ms intervals
moved Work from 900 to 2700 instead of the intended Case Studies landing at 1800.

Implemented and verified in the local production build:

- A same-direction wheel repeat can hurry the current move at a restrained 1.8×,
  but never queues another forward section while moving or settling. Keyboard
  presses and deliberate reversals retain the bounded latest-request behavior;
  navigation taps still avoid restarting entrances.
- Wheel grouping now requires 240ms quiet and rejects decreasing packets across
  gaps below 700ms. Tiny opposite-sign noise does not replace the last meaningful
  direction. Quick deliberate reversals require 24px or line/page wheel units.
  Wheel history survives completion so a tail crossing a landing stays grouped.
- A decaying first scroll retains its original authored pace. Hurrying cannot
  slow a timeline that an explicit keyboard action has already accelerated.
- Added `scripts/single-scroll-check.js`; expanded physical-stream unit fixtures
  and updated adaptive checks to require wheel repeats to stay at one boundary.
  All 21 input assertion groups pass, including gapped decaying tails crossing
  completion, equal-strength packets, noisy signs, unit modes and fresh gestures.

Final production evidence: `single-scroll-check.js` passed one-section Hero/Work
movement, a tail continuing after a chapter landed, reverse movement, equal
80ms wheel packets and a fresh gesture after quiet. The decaying first gesture
completed the full Work entrance in 4448ms, preserving its authored pace. The
reproduced Work 900 → 2700 bug now ends at the intended landing 1800.

Updated `adaptive-input-check.js` passed restrained wheel hurrying at one boundary,
explicit double-key navigation, closing seams, direction replacement, navigation
cancellation, same-target pending cancellation, double Back to top and footer
reverse recapture. A normal first entrance measured 4489.4ms; a deliberate second
wheel gesture shortened that same entrance to 2694.9ms while still stopping at
Work. Its 192-interval sample measured p95 17ms, with none above 32ms. The existing
full reverse/replay, interrupted-footer recovery, hovered preview coverage,
reverse geometry within 1px and responsive 320/390/768/1024px checks pass. Build,
TypeScript and whitespace checks pass. No runtime errors were found.

These are local Chrome lab results. Physical-device/Safari checks remain pending;
the conservative classifier is covered by replayed wheel traces rather than a
recording from the user's exact hardware. No dependencies, commits or deployment
were added. Refresh an already-open production preview tab to load this build.

## Earlier request: adapt to repeated scrolling and navigation input

The initial wheel policy and measurements in this section are historical and
superseded by the single-scroll correction above. Its tests missed real gapped,
decaying momentum; the keyboard/navigation/cancellation fixes remain relevant.

On 2026-10-24 the user requested faster, professional responses to deliberate
double scrolls, repeated navigation keys and double taps on navigation controls.
This supersedes the earlier rule that input during a playing beat is discarded.

Implemented and verified in the local production build:

- A fresh second gesture accelerates the active owner to 3× and retains one
  latest directional request for the next settled seam. Changing direction
  replaces that pending request. Events within one 50ms wheel burst are momentum;
  they do not multiply steps. Delivery waits 60ms after successful completion.
- `handoff.ts` coordinates intent/ownership only. Existing section owners expose
  their own `step` and `hurry` callbacks through `stageBeats.ts`; they retain all
  animation ownership. Successful completion and cancellation are separate paths.
- Hero owns the full nested Work entrance, so queued case travel cannot overlap
  cards still entering. Deep-link/focus restoration settles Work immediately.
  Case/closing playhead tweens are retained, accelerated and killed on reset.
- Navigation cancels old motion/requests; repeated navigation to the same landed
  destination does not rebuild its entrance. Footer release and Back to top can
  hurry their existing glides. Back to top also supports a faster second native
  activation. Touch scrolling and reduced-motion document flow remain native.
- Memoized key/wheel decisions prevent duplicate delivery across listeners.
  Editable controls, modified keys, native Space activation and pinch intent
  bypass the gesture gate. Auto-repeat cannot create an unbounded queue.
- `scripts/handoff-input-check.cjs` passes 10 assertion groups. Independent browser
  checks passed accelerated forward/reverse travel and a 130-event momentum burst
  advancing only one section. TypeScript and the production build pass.

Final production evidence: `scripts/adaptive-input-check.js` passed single and
double wheel input, double keyboard input through chapters/closing panels,
reverse travel, latest-direction replacement, cancellation during navigation,
same-target navigation at a pending seam, double Back to top, navigation during
the home glide and a queued reverse from the footer. Browser checks also confirm
pinch/zoom remains available during Hero and shared-frame motion; held keys stay
within held beats and remain native in the released footer.

The normal Hero-to-Work entrance measured 4471ms; two deliberate scrolls reached
the next Case Studies rest in 2375.4ms. Double Back to top measured 519.4ms.
The accelerated desktop sample had 143 RAF intervals, p95 16.9ms and none over
32ms. The existing hover regression passed again (65 frames, p95 19.2ms, none
over 32ms), as did interrupted-footer reverse recovery, full reverse/replay,
the shared preview covering its case slot and reversing within 1px, responsive
checks at 320/390/768/1024px, and navigation/history/reload/reduced-motion checks.
No runtime errors occurred in these checks.

Separate final checks passed reduced-motion activation with a move/next step
pending and subsequent reverse after re-entry. Touch emulation at 390×844 kept
native upward scrolling (1800 → 1515.33), readable cards, zero horizontal overflow
and a successful double native Back to top activation. These are local Chrome
lab results; physical phones and Safari remain untested. No dependencies,
commits or deployment were added.

